  "final_text": "All four steps are done, using only the Kibana tools.\n\n1. **Data view:** 'Kibana Sample Data Flights' (index `kibana_sample_data_flights`) has a time field named `timestamp`. Its fields include `Carrier` (string), `Cancelled` (boolean), `DestCityName`, `DistanceKilometers`, `AvgTicketPrice`, and `FlightDelay`.\n\n2. **Dashboard:** 'S4-f52e490d Flights' was created with two panels:\n   - \"Flights over time\": a line chart of count by `timestamp` (date histogram).\n   - \"Flights by carrier count\": a bar chart of count by `Carrier` (top 10 terms). I picked bar because you didn't specify a chart type.\n\n3. **Alert rule:** 'S4-f52e490d Cancelled flights' (id `0427c8d9-8350-42c7-a99c-19542cac4d98`) is an `.es-query` rule in `stackAlerts`, scheduled every 1m. It counts documents with `Cancelled: true` over a 5-minute window on `timestamp` and fires when the count is greater than 10. It was created disabled, so I enabled it.\n\n4. **Panel rename:** The carrier panel is now titled 'Flights by carrier'. I re-read the dashboard and the title is updated. Reading the rule back shows `enabled: true` (status `ok`).\n\nTIME_FIELD: timestamp\nRULE_ENABLED: yes",
